  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Starting from the Lavandou harbour, this coastal path follows the coast to La Fossette beach, with lovely views of the islands of Port-Cros and Levant. Easy as far as Saint-Clair beach — nicknamed "the pearl of Le Lavandou" for its turquoise water — it gets more demanding after that. Le Lavandou stretches over 12 km of coastline and counts 12 different fine-sand beaches: l'Anglade, the town-centre Grande Plage, Saint-Clair, La Fossette, Aiguebelle, Jean Blanc, l'Éléphant, Le Rossignol, Le Layet, Cavalière, Cap Nègre and Pramousquier (the last one shared with Rayol-Canadel-sur-Mer).</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>⚠️ Important: on the commune's beaches page, the town hall writes that access to the beaches is strictly forbidden to dogs and all animals from 1 April to 30 October, except dogs intended to assist people with disabilities. The tourist office FAQ gives the same rule, even on a leash, but up to 31 October. The La Fossette bathing water profile (2025) says for its part that domestic animals are prohibited in the bathing area, according to the municipal order on beach safety. On the La Fossette beach page, the tourist office writes that dogs are allowed from November to March.</p>
      <p>For the path itself, no official source read sets a precise rule for dogs: the tourist office listing puts the route under "Animals not accepted" in its list of services, without further explanation, whereas the beach page says "Animals accepted"; the pages of the official map say nothing about dogs. In the absence of a clear rule, keep your dog on a leash and avoid the beaches in season.</p>
      <p>The path starts at the Lavandou harbour, to the right of the tunnel, and the walk is 3 km each way (45 minutes according to the tourist office, 20 minutes to Saint-Clair), back the same way or along the cycle path beside the main road. The terrain is rock and sand, not suitable for pushchairs. The stretch between Saint-Clair and La Fossette is classed "reserved for experts": demanding difficulty level, technical passages not secured by handrails. The tourist office advises good shoes and, in high summer, a cap and water: adapt this for the dog, who walks on rock.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: save this walk for autumn, winter or very early spring if you want to enjoy the path with your dog without beach restrictions. If you really want a beach with your dog in high summer nearby, head to Tamaris beach in La Londe-les-Maures, Cabasson beach in Bormes-les-Mimosas (the pebble section to the west) or Ramatuelle's beaches — all named by Le Lavandou's own tourist office as alternatives.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>Le Lavandou and its harbour are right nearby, with views of the islands of Port-Cros and Levant offshore. Worth knowing: naturism is tolerated by municipal order on Le Layet and Le Rossignol beaches, further west.</p>
